1
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:14,474
This is an oil well with a downhole sucker-rod pump and a pumpjack on the surface.

2
00:00:14,974 --> 00:00:24,085
The principle is simple: a surface motor, through the pumpjack and a long string of rods, moves a plunger deep underground.

3
00:00:24,585 --> 00:00:32,067
Two check valves take turns letting fluid pass from the well into the pump, and then into the tubing.

4
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:38,196
Repeating this cycle over and over is what brings production to the surface.

5
00:00:40,096 --> 00:00:48,096
Let us look underground. The wellbore is supported by a steel casing string, with a cement sheath around it.

6
00:00:48,596 --> 00:00:55,856
Inside the casing hangs the production tubing. The produced fluid rises to the surface through it.

7
00:00:56,356 --> 00:01:03,022
Sucker rods move inside the tubing. At the bottom they are connected to the downhole pump.

8
00:01:03,522 --> 00:01:09,522
Lower still is the reservoir. It communicates with the well through perforations.

9
00:01:10,022 --> 00:01:18,393
There are two separate paths. The energy path: motor, gearbox, cranks, walking beam, polished rod, rods, plunger.

10
00:01:18,893 --> 00:01:26,078
And the fluid path: rock pores, perforations, pump intake, valves, tubing, wellhead and flowline.

11
00:01:26,578 --> 00:01:34,059
The rods only transmit force. The fluid rises through the space between the rods and the tubing wall.

12
00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:43,663
Oil does not sit in underground lakes. It is held in the pores and fractures of rock, such as sandstone.

13
00:01:44,163 --> 00:01:53,274
Between the mineral grains there are gaps and narrow channels. They can hold oil, formation water and gas at the same time.

14
00:01:53,774 --> 00:02:02,663
Porosity tells how much void space the rock contains. Permeability tells how easily fluids move through connected pores.

15
00:02:03,163 --> 00:02:11,015
A rock may hold a lot of oil yet give it up slowly if the channels between the pores conduct fluid poorly.

16
00:02:12,915 --> 00:02:17,915
What makes fluid move toward the well? A pressure difference.

17
00:02:18,415 --> 00:02:28,563
When reservoir pressure is higher than the pressure at the bottom of the well, fluids flow toward it. This difference is called drawdown.

18
00:02:29,063 --> 00:02:39,656
The pump removes the fluid that has entered the well. The fluid level and bottomhole pressure drop, and inflow from the reservoir is sustained.

19
00:02:40,156 --> 00:02:45,859
Note: the pump does not have to enter the rock. It works inside the wellbore.

20
00:02:47,759 --> 00:02:54,796
After drilling, a steel casing string is run into the hole and the space behind it is cemented.

21
00:02:55,296 --> 00:03:04,926
Then the casing is perforated opposite the reservoir: shaped charges punch channels through steel and cement and on into the rock.

22
00:03:05,426 --> 00:03:12,167
Now the path is open: from connected pores, along the perforation tunnels, into the casing.

23
00:03:12,667 --> 00:03:21,333
Here is a horizontal slice above the pump. From outside in: rock, cement, casing, annulus, tubing, fluid and the rod.

24
00:03:21,833 --> 00:03:30,574
The annulus is the gap between the tubing and the casing. Do not confuse it with the cement sheath outside the casing.

25
00:03:32,474 --> 00:03:41,141
The pump needs fluid arriving at sufficient pressure. After the well is shut in, a static fluid level is established.

26
00:03:41,641 --> 00:03:50,604
While the pump runs, the level drops to the dynamic level, until inflow from the reservoir equals the rate of withdrawal.

27
00:03:51,104 --> 00:03:59,104
As pressure falls, dissolved gas comes out of the oil. Bubbles are lighter than the liquid and tend to rise.

28
00:03:59,604 --> 00:04:07,900
A gas anchor makes the mixture change direction: liquid goes to the pump intake, while gas rises up the annulus.

29
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:12,993
The less gas enters the pump, the better it fills with liquid.

30
00:04:14,893 --> 00:04:21,781
Now the pump itself. Its body is a stationary barrel with a precisely machined inner surface.

31
00:04:22,281 --> 00:04:30,652
Inside it moves the plunger, connected to the rods. It is not solid: there is a passage through it for the fluid.

32
00:04:31,152 --> 00:04:38,781
At the bottom of the barrel sits the standing valve. It does not move, and it lets fluid into the pump.

33
00:04:39,281 --> 00:04:46,244
The plunger carries the traveling valve. It moves with the plunger and lets fluid pass upward.

34
00:04:46,744 --> 00:04:54,670
The volume between the standing valve and the plunger is the pump chamber. It changes as the plunger moves.

35
00:04:55,170 --> 00:05:02,504
Both are ball valves: a seat with a port, a ball, and a cage that limits how far the ball can lift.

36
00:05:03,004 --> 00:05:15,004
They have no actuator. Higher pressure below lifts the ball and lets the flow through. Higher pressure above pushes the ball onto its seat.

37
00:05:16,904 --> 00:05:22,681
Back to the surface. An electric motor turns the gearbox through a belt drive.

38
00:05:23,181 --> 00:05:30,219
The gearbox lowers the rotation speed and raises the torque. Its output shaft turns the cranks.

39
00:05:30,719 --> 00:05:39,163
Pitman arms link the cranks to the rear end of the walking beam. The beam rocks on a bearing atop the Samson post.

40
00:05:39,663 --> 00:05:46,996
The horsehead rises and falls. Its curved face keeps the bridle cables moving straight up and down.

41
00:05:47,496 --> 00:05:52,978
This is how rotation becomes the reciprocating motion of the polished rod.

42
00:05:53,478 --> 00:06:02,367
The rods and the fluid column are very heavy. Counterweights on the cranks descend as the rods go up, helping the motor.

43
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:10,274
On the downstroke the counterweights rise and store energy. The load on the drive becomes more even.

44
00:06:10,774 --> 00:06:21,070
At the wellhead the rod passes through a stuffing box. The seal keeps the fluid in, so it leaves through the side outlet into the flowline.

45
00:06:22,970 --> 00:06:31,637
Now the key part: the pump cycle. For simplicity, assume the pump is full of liquid and the valves are in good order.

46
00:06:32,137 --> 00:06:41,322
The plunger is at the bottom of its stroke. There is already fluid above it: the tubing is full all the way to the wellhead.

47
00:06:41,822 --> 00:06:50,489
Upstroke. The rods pull the plunger up. The traveling valve is closed, so the plunger carries the whole fluid column.

48
00:06:50,989 --> 00:06:59,878
The chamber under the plunger grows and its pressure falls. Intake pressure is now higher, and the standing valve opens.

49
00:07:00,378 --> 00:07:09,415
Two things happen at once: a new charge of fluid enters under the plunger, and the fluid above it is lifted up the tubing.

50
00:07:09,915 --> 00:07:14,915
No absolute vacuum is needed: a pressure difference is enough.

51
00:07:15,415 --> 00:07:20,415
Top of stroke. The plunger slows down and starts moving down.

52
00:07:20,915 --> 00:07:29,285
The chamber shrinks and its pressure rises. The standing valve closes and keeps fluid from returning to the well.

53
00:07:29,785 --> 00:07:37,933
For a short moment both valves are closed, until the pressure under the plunger exceeds the pressure above it.

54
00:07:38,433 --> 00:07:42,933
Downstroke. The traveling valve has opened.

55
00:07:43,433 --> 00:07:51,581
Fluid from the chamber passes through the valve seat and the plunger passage into the space above the plunger.

56
00:07:52,081 --> 00:08:01,119
The plunger in effect sinks through the fluid. The column in the tubing does not fall: the closed standing valve holds it.

57
00:08:01,619 --> 00:08:07,119
At the bottom the valves switch again, and the cycle repeats.

58
00:08:07,619 --> 00:08:25,619
In short: on the upstroke the pump takes in new fluid under the plunger and lifts the fluid above it. On the downstroke the standing valve holds the fluid while the traveling valve lets it pass above the plunger.

59
00:08:27,519 --> 00:08:38,926
One stroke does not carry a drop from the pump to the wellhead. Each cycle adds a new charge to the tubing and pushes everything above it a little higher.

60
00:08:39,426 --> 00:08:46,685
Let us follow one charge in fast-forward. It takes hundreds of strokes to travel the whole string.

61
00:08:47,185 --> 00:08:55,630
The pump has to overcome the weight of this column. A 1,000-metre column of liquid presses down with about 88 bar.

62
00:08:56,130 --> 00:09:01,537
As the fluid rises, pressure drops; gas comes out of the oil and expands.

63
00:09:03,437 --> 00:09:09,437
At the surface, the mixture leaves through the wellhead outlet into the flowline.

64
00:09:09,937 --> 00:09:17,419
Next come a metering unit and a separator. Here gas is separated from the liquid, and oil from water.

65
00:09:17,919 --> 00:09:25,104
Oil goes on to treatment and transport, water to treatment and disposal, and gas to its own line.

66
00:09:27,004 --> 00:09:35,596
A real well is more complicated. A rod string a kilometre long is elastic: it stretches and contracts like a spring.

67
00:09:36,096 --> 00:09:42,244
So the plunger stroke downhole differs from the polished-rod stroke at the surface.

68
00:09:42,744 --> 00:09:55,744
Theoretical displacement is plunger area times stroke length times pumping speed. A 44 mm plunger, a 2 m stroke and 6 strokes per minute give about 26 cubic metres per day.

69
00:09:56,244 --> 00:10:02,244
Actual output is lower because of gas, leakage and incomplete filling.

70
00:10:02,744 --> 00:10:18,744
Gas is compressible. With a lot of gas in the chamber, the downstroke first just compresses it, so the traveling valve opens late. In a severe case it never opens: this is gas lock.

71
00:10:19,244 --> 00:10:31,244
And if the pump takes more than the reservoir delivers, the chamber fills only partly. The plunger hits the liquid: this is fluid pound.

72
00:10:31,744 --> 00:10:43,244
That is why operation is monitored. Sensors measure polished-rod load and position and plot a dynamometer card. Its shape shows how well the pump is filling.

73
00:10:43,744 --> 00:10:49,448
A controller then adjusts the speed or pauses the unit to let fluid build up.

74
00:10:51,348 --> 00:11:01,422
To sum up: the motor turns the cranks, the walking beam rocks, the plunger moves up and down, and two valves turn that motion into flow.

75
00:11:01,922 --> 00:11:07,181
Stroke by stroke, oil makes its way from the rock pores to the surface.
